{
  "name": "regex-utf16-units-backreferences",
  "description": "Without `u`, Dart's RegExp (ECMAScript) reads a string as UTF-16 code units, so the emoji \ud83d\ude00 (U+1F600) is two units, a surrogate pair; with `u` it is one code point. With `g` on \ud83d\ude00: `.`, `[^a]` and `\\S` match each unit, giving \"##\"; `.{2}` takes both units as one match, so the replacement \"[{{value}}]\" gives \"[\ud83d\ude00]\"; in `^(..)$` the group holds both units, so \"<$1>\" gives \"<\ud83d\ude00>\"; the class `[\ud83d\ude00]` holds the two units and gives \"##\"; and `x*` with an empty replacement gives \ud83d\ude00 back whole. `\ud83d\ude00+` repeats only the second unit, so on \"\ud83d\ude00\ud83d\ude00\" it matches twice, \"##\". `(?<=.).` on \"a\ud83d\ude00b\" replaces each of the three units after the a, \"a###\". The `matches` BoolExpr op compiles its regex with no flags: `^.$` does not match \ud83d\ude00 (\"no\") and `^..$` does (\"yes\"). Without `u` a letter above U+FFFF does not fold: the Deseret capital long I \ud801\udc00 (U+10400) with `gi` on its small letter \ud801\udc28 (U+10428) matches nothing; with `giu` it gives \"#\". With `u`, after an empty match at a pair's high half the next search starts after the whole pair, so `x*` with `gu` on \ud83d\ude00 gives \"#\ud83d\ude00#\". Under `gi` a back reference compares letters as `i` does without `u`, by each letter's uppercase, keeping a letter as itself when its uppercase is not one character or is ASCII while the letter is not: `(s)\\1` on \"s\u017f\" (the long s U+017F, uppercase S), `(K)\\1` on \"K\u212a\" (the Kelvin sign U+212A, its own uppercase) and `(.)\\1` on \"\u00df\u1e9e\" (U+00DF, uppercase \"SS\", and U+1E9E) match nothing, while `(s)\\1` on \"sS\" gives \"#\". With `giu` simple case folding applies (U+017F to s, U+212A to k, U+1E9E to U+00DF) and those three give \"#\". No row's result splits a pair. Every replace-mode regex puts \"#\" for each match unless its replacement is named above. Every input reads page state, so the engine keeps the Values unresolved with `watches` and the client resolves them.",
